o d can be from 3 to 11. For each d, find integer solutions (x,y) with x>=1, y>=1, x+y <=6.\n\n- d=3: 2x + y = 3 => possible x=1 => y=1 => (1,1) sum=2 <=6 => valid. x=0 not allowed. So only (1,1) => 1 point.\n\n- d=4: 2x + y = 4 => x=1 => y=2 => (1,2) sum=3 <=6 => valid. x=2 => y=0 invalid. So 1 point.\n\n- d=5: 2x + y = 5 => x=1 => y=3 => (1,3) sum=4 <=6 => valid. x=2 => y=1 => (2,1) sum=3 <=6 => valid. So 2 points.\n\n- d=6: 2x + y = 6 => x=1 => y=4 => (1,4) sum=5 <=6 => valid. x=2 => y=2 => (2,2) sum=4 <=6 => valid. x=3 => y=0 invalid. So 2 points.\n\n- d=7: 2x + y = 7 => x=1 => y=5 => (1,5) sum=6 <=6 => valid. x=2 => y=3 => (2,3) sum=5 <=6 => valid. x=3 => y=1 => (3,1) sum=4 <=6 => valid. So 3 points! Indeed (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). So line 2x + y = 7 has 3 points in T_5.\n\n- d=8: 2x + y = 8 => x=1 => y=6 => (1,6) sum=7 >6 invalid. x=2 => y=4 => (2,4) sum=6 <=6 => valid. x=3 => y=2 => (3,2) sum=5 <=6 => valid. x=4 => y=0 invalid. So 2 points.\n\n- d=9: 2x + y = 9 => x=2 => y=5 => (2,5) sum=7 >6 invalid. x=3 => y=3 => (3,3) sum=6 <=6 => valid. x=4 => y=1 => (4,1) sum=5 <=6 => valid. x=1 => y=7 invalid. So 2 points.\n\n- d=10: 2x + y = 10 => x=3 => y=4 => (3,4) sum=7 >6 invalid. x=4 => y=2 => (4,2) sum=6 <=6 => valid. x=5 => y=0 invalid. So 1 point.\n\n- d=11: 2x + y = 11 => x=5 => y=1 => (5,1) sum=6 <=6 => valid. x=4 => y=3 => (4,3) sum=7 >6 invalid. So 1 point.\n\nThus line 2x + y = 7 yields 3 points. That's the line y = -2x + 7. In the solution, they gave line y = -2x + 5 for k=3. For k=5, the line y = -2x + 7 yields 3 points. So slope -2 line can achieve 3 points for k=5 as well. Wait earlier we thought slope -2 line only yields 2 points for k=5. Let's double-check: The line y = -2x + 5 gave points (1,3),(2,1). That's 2 points. But line y = -2x + 7 gives points (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Indeed those are three points. So slope -2 line can achieve 3 points for k=5. So the earlier analysis was incomplete. So there are at least two lines that achieve 3 points: slope 1 line (y