pe -2 line (p=-2,q=1) can achieve the bound for k=5 (3 points). Indeed, p+q = -1, but the bound is floor((k+1)/2) = 3, and we found a line with 3 points. So the bound is not only achieved by lines with |p+q| = 2; lines with |p+q| = 1 can also achieve the bound for certain k. The solution's claim that only lines with |p+q| = 2 achieve the bound is false. However, they later claim that the only lines that can contain m+1 points are the three lines (10). That is false for k=5 because slope -2 line also contains 3 points. But they might have considered that slope -2 line is one of the three lines (they listed p=-2,q=1 as one of the three). Indeed, they listed three possibilities for |p+q| = 2: p=q=1 (slope 1), p=-2,q=1 (slope -2), p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2). Wait p=-2,q=1 yields p+q = -1, not 2. But they said \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities: p=q=1 (slope 1); p=-2,q=1 (slope -2); p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2).\" Let's check: For p=-2,q=1, p+q = -1, absolute value = 1, not 2. So they made a mistake: they incorrectly identified p=-2,q=1 as having |p+q|=2. Actually p+q = -1, |p+q| = 1. So they mischaracterized the case. However, they earlier derived bound (2.5) for any sunny line, and they said the bound is sharp (see §4). In §4 they claim that the three lines listed achieve the bound. Indeed, slope -2 line does achieve the bound for k=3 (3 points? Actually for k=3, bound is floor((4)/2)=2, slope -2 line yields 2 points). For k=5, slope -2 line yields 3 points, which matches bound. So slope -2 line is indeed a line that achieves bound, but it's not in the case |p+q|=2; it's in case |p+q|=1 with q=1. So the classification is wrong. But does this affect the final conclusion? Let's see.\n\nThe solution's argument for impossibility of k>3 relies on the claim that only three lines can achieve the bound, and they are pairwise disjoint only for k <= 3. However, we have found that for k=5, there are at least three lines that achieve bound (the same three lines). Are ther